        "thinking": "The sidecar is still receiving the install request as an npm install (`isLocalPath: false`) even though we're proxying with `isLocalPath: true` + absolute path from the examples list.\n\nWait — the error says `npm install @paperclipai/plugin-file-browser-example` which means it's still doing an npm install. This means the `isLocalPath` rewrite is still happening.\n\nLet me trace through:\n1. UI sends `POST /api/plugins/install { packageName: \"/Users/migmac/.../plugin-file-browser-example\", isLocalPath: true }` (from the sidecar-provided examples with absolute path)\n\n\n2. The CF install handler checks if it's an npm package by evaluating whether `isLocalPath` is false or the path doesn't start with \"/\" — but since both conditions are false here, `isNpmPackage` should be false, meaning the body shouldn't be rewritten and should keep the local path intact.\n\nSo why is the sidecar still receiving an npm install request?\n\nActually, I think I see the issue now — the sidecar is the full Node.js server that CF is proxying to at `http://127.0.0.1:3101`, and it's getting the request with the absolute path and `isLocalPath: true`. But the error message shows it's trying to run `npm install @paperclipai/plugin-file-browser-example`, which is the package name, not the path. That means somewhere the packageName is being set to the npm package name instead of the local path.\n\nLooking at the timestamp of the error (`21_20_25`), it's very recent — right after I made the commit to proxy examples through the sidecar. The sidecar's `listBundledPluginExamples()` should be returning absolute paths in the `localPath` field, but the error suggests the user might still be running old code where `localPath` was just the package name, or the sidecar response is returning something unexpected.\n\nActually, I think I see what's happening. The error shows `npm install @paperclipai/plugin-file-browser-example`, which means the old install handler was treating the package name as a remote dependency and trying to install it via npm. In the old version, we'd set `isLocalPath: false` for anything that didn't start with `/`, and the sidecar would attempt an npm install. With my latest fix, the examples endpoint now returns absolute paths from the sidecar, so the install handler should recognize these as local paths instead.\n\nBut the user is still seeing the old npm install error, which means they haven't restarted their dev server yet to pick up the new code. Let me trace through what should happen with the fix in place: the UI gets examples with absolute paths like `/Users/migmac/.../plugin-file-browser-example`, sends that as the packageName with `isLocalPath: true`, and the install handler should keep it as a local path instead of trying npm install.\n\nActually, I'm realizing there might be another issue with how the proxy request is being constructed and sent to the sidecar...\n\nSetting up the proxy call...\n\nWhen the request goes through the Durable Object stub, it gets routed to `http://sidecar/api/plugins/install`, and then the SidecarContainer falls back to the local sidecar URL at `http://127.0.0.1:3101`. But I'm not entirely sure what endpoint the sidecar is actually listening on for the install request — I need to verify what the sidecar server is actually running and whether it's properly handling that POST endpoint.\n\nLooking at the dev setup, running `bun run dev:cf` only starts the CF Worker on port 8787, but the sidecar (a separate Node.js companion server) also needs to be running on port 3101 for the proxying to work. If the sidecar isn't running, the connection will fail.\n\nThe user is getting npm 404 errors rather than connection refused, which suggests they're still running an older build where the package name wasn't being converted to a proper path. They need to rebuild and restart the dev server, and make sure the sidecar is also running alongside it.",
